@page "/pager"

<CatHead Title="Pagination and breadcrumbs">
    Moving through a long result set, and saying where the current page sits.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
        <strong>Pages are addresses.</strong> Use <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> for every page that
        exists, not <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> — a page of results should be linkable, openable in a
        new tab and reachable with the back button. Only the disabled ends are buttons or spans.
    </div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pager.Pagination" Title="Pagination" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Wrap it in <code>&lt;nav aria-label="Pagination"&gt;</code> and put
        <code>aria-current="page"</code> on the current one. The class colours it;
        <code>aria-current</code> is what is announced. <code>.page-count</code> pushes the range
        to the far end, which is where the reader looks to find out how much is left.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pager.PaginationViewState" Title="When the page is view state" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The rule above holds wherever a page of results <em>is</em> an address. It does not hold
        when the page number is view state rather than a location — a Blazor Server table whose
        page, sort and filters live in the circuit. <code>/employees/all</code> is one address;
        page 4 of a 27-column table sorted by cost centre is not an address the app has, and
        inventing query-string routing for it means re-reading and re-validating four parameters on
        every navigation to gain a link nobody sends.
    </p>
    <p>
        So: <code>&lt;button class="page-link" type="button"&gt;</code> throughout, and
        <code>disabled</code> on the ends rather than <code>aria-disabled</code> — a real
        <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> has the attribute, which is announced and removes it from the
        tab order. The stylesheet already styles both forms; nothing changes but the element.
    </p>
    <p>
        Judge it by whether the page has an address, not by which is easier to write. If a reader
        could reasonably send someone a link to page 4, use the anchors above.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pager.Breadcrumbs" Title="Breadcrumbs" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For where a page sits when the sidebar cannot show it — a record three levels inside a
        section. If the sidebar already highlights the page, a breadcrumb repeats it and earns
        nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
        The separator is a pseudo-element, so it is never selected when the trail is copied and
        never read out. The last item is the current page: mark it
        <code>aria-current="page"</code> and leave it a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, not a link to
        where you already are. Long trails scroll, and it is the leading edge that gets cut,
        because the levels nearest the current page are the useful ones.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pager.PagerCompact" Title="Prev, next and the range" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Enough for a short list, and for a pager that has to live inside a
        <a href="/toolbar">toolbar</a> row. <code>.page-count</code> is what makes it usable without
        numbers: &ldquo;Next&rdquo; alone does not say how much is left.
    </p>
    <p>
        The disabled ends are <code>&lt;span aria-disabled="true"&gt;</code>, not links to nowhere. A
        page that does not exist has no address, so there is nothing to link to.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Pager.BreadcrumbAbovePageHead" Title="Above the page head" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Where a breadcrumb belongs: above the heading, so the trail is read before the title it leads
        to. <a href="/structure"><code>.page-head</code></a> then carries the title, the supporting
        line and the actions that apply to the whole page.
    </p>
    <p>
        One <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> per page, and it is this one. The breadcrumb is navigation, not a
        heading level.
    </p>
</CatExample>
